\par Let T1 and T2 be the two trees.
\par  
\par compare the root element of the two trees
\par Tree *Merge(Tree *T1,Tree *T2)
\par \{
\par // of course u can add null as the terminating condition
\par if(T1->el < T2->el)
\par \{
\par   Tree *x=T1->right;
\par   T1->right=T2;
\par   T2->left=Merge(x,T2->left);
\par   return T1;
\par \}
\par else
\par \{
\par   // Similarly the other half can be added
\par  
\par \}
\par \}
\par 
\par --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
\par Q. Suppose you have an array of 1001 integers. The integers are in random order, but you know each of the integers is between 1 and 1000 (inclusive). 
\par In addition, each number appears only once in the array, except for one number, which occurs twice. Assume that you can access each element of 
\par the array only once. Describe an algorithm to find the repeated number? 
\par If you used auxiliary storage in your algorithm, can you find an algorithm that does not require it?  
\par  
\par sol: sum of 1000 integers are = 1000 * (1000+1)/2 = 500500. Now to find the sum of given array numers ..and  subtract 500500 from that sum..
\par        this should give us the repeated number...
\par sol:
\par   for(int i=1;i<=1001;i++)
\par     \{
\par     int val = array[i];
\par     if(array[val % 1001] > 1001)
\par       print ( value at i is repeated );
\par     else
\par       array[val % 1001] += 1001;
\par     \}
\par  
\par This will work even when there are }{\b\f1\fs20\insrsid15272566 multiple elements repeated multiple times}{\f1\fs20\insrsid15272566 .  The original array can be restored by doing mod 1001.
\par It works, but it also violates the rule about accessing each array element only once. 
